<section>
  <div class="container content">
    <h2>Pricing & Ordering</h2>
    
    <h3>How much does a gold wash plant cost?</h3>
    <p>Gold wash plant prices vary by capacity and customization. Our 50-ton entry-level plants are the most affordable, with 100-ton, 200-ton, and 300-ton models priced accordingly. Every plant is custom-built to your specifications, so pricing depends on your specific configuration. <a href="/contact/">Contact us for a free custom quote</a>.</p>
    
    <h3>Do you offer financing?</h3>
    <p>We can work with you on payment plans for qualifying orders. Contact our sales team to discuss financing options for your operation.</p>
    
    <h3>How long does it take to build?</h3>
    <p>Most wash plants are built in 1-3 weeks depending on the configuration. Custom modifications may add additional time. We'll give you an exact timeline when you place your order.</p>
    
    <h2>Shipping & Delivery</h2>
    
    <h3>Where do you ship?</h3>
    <p>Everywhere. We ship worldwide — proven on 6 continents including <a href="/locations/africa/">Africa</a>, <a href="/locations/australia/">Australia</a>, South America, Asia, and beyond. We handle all logistics and can deliver to remote mining sites.</p>
    
    <h3>How is the equipment shipped?</h3>
    <p>Domestic orders ship via flatbed truck. International orders are container-ready and ship via ocean freight. We coordinate with carriers experienced in heavy equipment delivery.</p>
    
    <h2>Setup & Operation</h2>
    
    <h3>How long does setup take?</h3>
    <p>30 minutes or less. Level the plant, connect your water source, and start feeding. No special tools or training required. <a href="/how-it-works/">Learn more about how it works</a>.</p>
    
    <h3>What do I need to run the wash plant?</h3>
    <p>A water source (creek, pond, or pump), a way to feed material (excavator or loader), and fuel for the engine. That's it. Our all-in-one design means no additional screens or sluice boxes are needed.</p>
    
    <h3>How many people are needed to operate it?</h3>
    <p>One person can run the entire system. One operates the excavator to feed material, and the wash plant runs itself. Many operations run with 1-2 people.</p>
    
    <h2>Performance & Recovery</h2>
    
    <h3>What is the gold recovery rate?</h3>
    <p>95-98% recovery rate, including fine gold down to 200 mesh (0.075mm). Our scientifically-designed sluice system captures gold that many competitors miss.</p>
    
    <h3>Does it work with fine gold?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Our sluice design is specifically engineered to capture fine gold. Many of our customers in <a href="/locations/alaska/">Alaska</a> and <a href="/locations/california/">California</a> report excellent fine gold recovery. Read more about <a href="/blog/fine-gold-recovery/">fine gold recovery</a>.</p>
    
    <h3>Is the equipment mercury-free?</h3>
    <p>100% mercury-free. Our wash plants use only water and gravity for gold recovery. Environmentally responsible mining that meets all state and federal regulations.</p>
    
    <h2>Equipment & Sizing</h2>
    
    <h3>What size wash plant do I need?</h3>
    <p>It depends on your expected throughput. Our <a href="/products/50-ton/">50-ton</a> is ideal for small operations, the <a href="/products/100-ton/">100-ton</a> for medium operations, and the <a href="/products/200-ton/">200-ton</a> and <a href="/products/300-ton/">300-ton</a> for commercial-scale mining. Contact us and we'll recommend the perfect size.</p>
    
    <h3>What makes your wash plants different from other equipment?</h3>
    <p>Our wash plants are all-in-one systems — they handle screening, classification, and gold recovery in a single portable unit. You don't need separate equipment. Just hook up water, feed material, and start recovering gold.</p>
    
    <h3>How does your equipment compare to competitors?</h3>
    <p>We offer higher recovery rates, faster setup, and American-made quality at competitive prices. <a href="/compare/">See our detailed comparison</a> against the competition.</p>
  </div>
</section>

